+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report: `snap`
+
+Hegel tests were added to the existing test file `test/tests.rs` (module
+`tests::hegel_props`), alongside the quickcheck tests they port/extend.
+Dependency added with `cargo add --dev hegeltest -p snap-test`
+(hegeltest v0.28.2). Run with `cargo test -p snap-test`.
+
+All tests share a structured payload generator, `snappy_input`, drawing from
+five payload families: arbitrary bytes, single-byte runs (up to 70,000 bytes,
+crossing the 65,536-byte `MAX_BLOCK_SIZE`), repeated seed chunks (up to
+~256KiB, multi-block), Unicode text, and low-entropy bytes over a 1-4 symbol
+alphabet (partial matches / short copies).
+
+## Properties tested
+
+1. **`hegel_roundtrip_raw`** — raw `Encoder` then `Decoder` is the identity.
+   Evidence: port of `qc_roundtrip` (case count 1000 carried over) and the
+   `testtrip!` corpus tests; `Encoder::compress` docs say "`input` can be any
+   arbitrary sequence of bytes".
+2. **`hegel_roundtrip_frame_chunked_writes`** — `write::FrameEncoder` then
+   `read::FrameDecoder` is the identity for *any* split of the input across
+   `write_all` calls with drawn interleaved `flush()`es. Evidence: port of
+   `qc_roundtrip_stream` (1000 cases carried over), strengthened — the
+   original used one `write_all`, no flushes, and discarded empty inputs.
+3. **`hegel_read_write_frame_encoders_agree`** — `read::FrameEncoder` and
+   `write::FrameEncoder` produce byte-identical compressed output for a
+   one-shot write. Evidence: generalizes the per-corpus
+   `read_and_write_frame_encoder_match` tests.
+4. **`hegel_frame_decoder_small_buffers`** — decoding through `Read::read`
+   with a drawn caller buffer size (1..=97) equals whole-input decoding.
+   Evidence: `FrameDecoder` implements `io::Read`, whose contract permits any
+   buffer size; gap identified in streaming coverage.
+5. **`hegel_read_frame_encoder_small_buffers`** — reading compressed bytes
+   from `read::FrameEncoder` with a drawn small buffer equals `read_to_end`.
+   Evidence: generalizes `read_frame_encoder_big_and_little_buffers`, whose
+   comment notes the encoder "uses different code paths depending on buffer
+   size" (it tested only sizes 5 and 1,000,000 on one corpus file).
+6. **`hegel_raw_decompress_never_panics`** — `decompress_len` and
+   `Decoder::decompress` return `Err` or `Ok` but never panic, on arbitrary
+   bytes and on valid compressions with one drawn bit flipped. Evidence: the
+   `testerrored!` suite shows malformed input is expected to produce typed
+   `Error`s, never crashes. (The output buffer is capped at 16MiB to protect
+   the *test's* memory — a corrupt header can claim 4GiB; the undersized
+   `BufferTooSmall` path is exercised instead in that case.)
+7. **`hegel_frame_decoder_never_panics`** — `read::FrameDecoder::read_to_end`
+   never panics on arbitrary bytes, arbitrary bytes behind a valid stream
+   identifier (so drawn bytes reach chunk parsing), or a valid stream with
+   one bit flipped. Evidence: `read.rs` returns typed errors for every
+   reserved/invalid chunk case; issue #42 regression (`test_short_input`)
+   shows EOF must be an error, not a panic.
+8. **`hegel_frame_truncation_prefix_or_error`** — decoding a truncated valid
+   frame stream either errors or yields a prefix of the original data.
+   Evidence: chunks are self-contained (`read.rs` reads chunk-by-chunk), so
+   truncation at a chunk boundary is a legal shorter stream; mid-chunk
+   truncation hits `read_exact` → `UnexpectedEof`.
+9. **`hegel_decompress_len_matches_original`** — `decompress_len(press(d)) ==
+   d.len()`. Evidence: `decompress_len` docs ("Returns the decompressed size
+   (in bytes) of the compressed bytes given").
+10. **`hegel_max_compress_len_bound`** — compressed output length never
+    exceeds `max_compress_len(input.len())`, and the bound is nonzero for
+    all valid input sizes. Evidence: `Encoder::compress` docs require
+    `output` of exactly this size; `compress_vec` allocates it.
+11. **`hegel_concatenated_frames`** — concatenating two encoded frame streams
+    decodes to the concatenated payloads. Evidence: the framing format allows
+    repeated stream identifiers; `read.rs`'s `Ok(ChunkType::Stream)` branch
+    accepts and validates a mid-stream identifier after the first chunk.
+12. **`hegel_encoder_reuse_deterministic`** — compressing with a reused
+    `Encoder` gives the same bytes as a fresh `Encoder` for each of a drawn
+    sequence of inputs. Evidence: docs say "It is beneficial to reuse an
+    Encoder when possible", implying reuse must not change output; the
+    encoder carries hash-table state (`small`, `big`) across calls that must
+    be properly reset/overwritten.
+
+## Test results
+
+- Full suite: `cargo test -p snap-test` — **94 passed, 0 failed** (82
+  pre-existing + 12 hegel).
+- Liveness check: `hegel_decompress_len_matches_original` was temporarily
+  inverted (`data.len() + 1`); hegel failed and shrank the counterexample to
+  `data = []`. Reverted; `.hegel/` failure database deleted afterwards.
+- Exploratory high-count run: all 12 hegel tests run once at 10x case count
+  (1000/10000) — all passed in under a second. Committed tests use the
+  default 100 cases, except the two quickcheck ports which keep the
+  original 1000.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+None found. All properties held at 10x case counts, including bit-flip and
+truncation robustness on both the raw and frame decoders.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **"Corrupted input must error"** (validation/rejection converse): not
+  asserted. A bit flip in a raw-format literal body yields different but
+  *valid* Snappy data, and in the frame format a flipped chunk-type byte can
+  turn a data chunk into a reserved-skippable chunk (0x80–0xFD) that decodes
+  successfully with different output. Only no-panic (and, for truncation,
+  prefix-or-error) is sound.
+- **"read/write FrameEncoders agree under chunked writes"**: the byte-level
+  equivalence (property 3) is only sound for a one-shot write — intermediate
+  flushes change block boundaries, producing different (still equivalent)
+  compressed bytes. The chunked-write case is covered by the roundtrip
+  property 2 instead.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- The porting guidance (enumerate old properties first, carry over case
+  counts, strengthen weak ports) mapped cleanly onto this quickcheck suite.
+- The "protect the test's resources, not the library's contract" rule was
+  directly relevant: `decompress_vec` on corrupt headers would allocate up
+  to 4GiB in the *test*; the reference's suggested comment convention made
+  the 16MiB cap self-documenting.
+- `#[hegel::composite]` + imperative `match` on a drawn discriminant was a
+  pleasant way to build the multi-family payload generator; no `one_of!`
+  type-unification issues.
+- One paper cut: the reference suggests importing
+  `hegel::generators::{self, Generator}`, but with no combinator methods
+  used the `Generator` import triggers `unused_imports` — the reference does
+  warn about this, which saved time.
